WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.095 --> 00:00:01.251
(suspenseful atmospheric music)

00:00:01.251 --> 00:00:03.276
- I had two goals setting
out on this expedition.

00:00:03.276 --> 00:00:05.868
One was to be the first person

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:09.276
to ride the entire length
of the Ho Chi Minh Trail.

00:00:09.276 --> 00:00:10.740
The second was a personal goal

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.560
and that was to find the place

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.810
where my dad's plane went down in 1972.

00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:23.544
Blood Road is sort of a slang name

00:00:23.544 --> 00:00:24.776
for the Ho Chi Minh Trail.

00:00:24.776 --> 00:00:26.600
And the Ho Chi Minh Trail was the supply route

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.569
used during the Vietnam War

00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:31.907
for the North Vietnamese to supply arms

00:00:31.907 --> 00:00:34.770
and troops towards the south.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.201
The whole route was about
1,200 miles from top to bottom.

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:40.377
It's the longest ride I've ever done,

00:00:40.377 --> 00:00:42.336
completely unknown territory.

00:00:42.336 --> 00:00:43.753
(poignant music)

00:00:43.753 --> 00:00:45.687
My dad served in the Vietnam War

00:00:45.687 --> 00:00:48.104
and he was shot down in 1972.

00:00:50.487 --> 00:00:52.927
I was just three years old when he died,

00:00:52.927 --> 00:00:57.477
and so I don't have any of my
own personal memories of him.

00:00:57.477 --> 00:01:00.358
I was looking for a
way to get to know him,

00:01:00.358 --> 00:01:01.828
a man that I never knew,

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:04.234
and physically going to the place

00:01:04.234 --> 00:01:06.729
and connecting by bicycle was really

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:08.673
the only way I knew how.

00:01:08.673 --> 00:01:11.827
The train was a total mixed bag

00:01:11.827 --> 00:01:13.525
from pavement in the cities

00:01:13.525 --> 00:01:14.942
to using machete.

00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:17.409
Plenty of times we had to put our bikes

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:20.574
on the shoulders, and carry
'em up and over a mountain,

00:01:20.574 --> 00:01:23.838
but I will say the physical
part of the journey

00:01:23.838 --> 00:01:25.221
wasn't the most challenging part

00:01:25.221 --> 00:01:26.625
of the journey for me.

00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:29.815
For me, it really was
opening up emotionally

00:01:29.815 --> 00:01:31.565
and being vulnerable.

00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:35.657
There's a huge trench as far as I can see,

00:01:35.657 --> 00:01:38.240
just down where the plane slid.

00:01:41.168 --> 00:01:42.814
It was the first time in my life

00:01:42.814 --> 00:01:45.566
that I felt my dad's presence,

00:01:45.566 --> 00:01:46.624
I felt close to him

00:01:46.624 --> 00:01:47.870
and I felt like he was there.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.262
And the first time in my life

00:01:50.262 --> 00:01:52.472
I felt like I could stop chasing something

00:01:52.472 --> 00:01:56.174
and I could just sit,
and be in that moment.

00:01:56.174 --> 00:01:57.342
As a professional athlete,

00:01:57.342 --> 00:02:00.238
I'm trained to be strong and independent

00:02:00.238 --> 00:02:02.245
and show no weakness.

00:02:02.245 --> 00:02:03.358
It took me by surprise

00:02:03.358 --> 00:02:05.858
that it impacted me that much.

